.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_CC_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_cc_scoped \- declare and create an ordered map with a custom comparison procedure, destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t, custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle with the name hm_name. It also declares the
companion type variables. Those variables carry key_t and val_t to every
later type-inferred macro. The macro then creates a map. custom_comparison_proc
orders the keys of that map, and not the default order for key_t. The macro
also attaches the cleanup attribute. That attribute destroys the map on
every exit from the enclosing scope, an early return included.

The macro is cbmap_construct_cc(3) plus that cleanup attribute. Nothing else
differs. cbmap_construct_scoped(3) is the same macro with the default order.
cbmap_construct_mp_scoped(3) takes an allocator in place of a comparison
procedure, and cbmap_construct_full_scoped(3) takes both.

A struct key_t needs this scoped variant. The default order for a key type
that the map does not recognize is a raw comparison of the representation of
the key.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                   Key type. It tells every later macro how to read
                        a key. It also tells the container which width to
                        expect and which alignment to give a stored key
val_t                   Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read it.
                        The library never gives it to the container
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for key_t
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure gets pointers to two keys. It returns a negative value when
the first key orders before the second key. It returns a positive value when
the first key orders after the second key. It returns zero when the map must
treat the two keys as the same key. A zero makes a call an update and not an
insert. The procedure therefore defines key equality and key order.

The procedure must give a strict weak order over key_t. That order must not
change while the map holds elements. The procedure gets two bare pointers
and no lengths. It can only read a width that key_t fixes. The map
therefore validates the width of an incoming key against key_t. The map
also aligns its stored keys for key_t.

The procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map. That life
ends here at the closing brace of the enclosing scope.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
The macro needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
The macro uses the default allocator
The walk visits the elements in ascending key order, by the order of this
procedure. cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract
The exit of the scope destroys the map and clears the handle. You can
therefore also call cbmap_destroy(3) earlier in the scope
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle. A function
that receives the handle therefore needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return it. Do not store it in
a place that outlives the scope. Both actions give out a handle whose map
the cleanup attribute frees at the exit of the scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct { int major; int minor; } version;
int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b);

void summarize(version *v, size_t n) {
    cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(releases, version, int, version_cmp);
    int one = 1;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cbmap_insert(releases, v[i], one);
    }
}                             /* The macro destroys releases here */
.fi
